module Core

### Multi-threaded atomic reference-counting controller for immutable shared data.
### Used by value-typed Text/Bytes/Integer/Decimal/Complex to share an immutable
### payload across threads with O(1) refcount-bump copy semantics.
###
### Differs from `RetainController[T]`:
###   - strong-only (no weak refs) — immutable shared data has no need for `Tracked`.
###   - atomic counter — safe to copy/drop across threads.
###   - no `data` field — the payload lives in the owning handle struct alongside
###     a `Hijacked[FrozenController]` controller pointer. The handle
###     decides what to deallocate when the count hits zero.
entity FrozenController
  ### Stores the strong reference count. Accessed via SeqCst atomic intrinsics
  ### driven directly on the field's live heap address (see note below).
  secret strong_count: U64

### Creates a new controller with one strong reference.
### :returns: The resulting `FrozenController` value.
dangerous routine FrozenController() -> FrozenController
  return FrozenController(
    strong_count: 1_u64
  )

# NOTE: We drive the SeqCst atomic intrinsics directly on `me.strong_count`'s
# live heap address. Records are value types with no by-ref receiver, so a
# value-typed atomic-cell method would receive `me` by value and operate on a
# *stack copy* — the RMW would never reach the real counter (frozen at 1 →
# double-free on every drop). `me` here is an entity (by-ref ptr), so
# `me.strong_count.hijack()` yields the field's live lvalue address and the
# intrinsics mutate the genuine shared counter.

### Atomically increments the strong reference count when a handle is cloned.
dangerous routine FrozenController.hold()
  discard LLVM::atomic_add(ptr: me.strong_count.hijack(), value: 1_u64)
  return

### Atomically decrements the strong reference count when a handle is dropped.
### :returns: The previous count. The caller deallocates the payload + controller
###          when this returns `1` (i.e. the cell is now zero).
dangerous routine FrozenController.unhold() -> U64
  return LLVM::atomic_sub[U64](ptr: me.strong_count.hijack(), value: 1_u64)

### Returns the current number of live strong handles.
### Snapshot only — value may have changed by the time the caller observes it.
### :returns: The current strong reference count.
@readonly
dangerous routine FrozenController.strong_count() -> U64
  return LLVM::atomic_load[U64](ptr: me.strong_count.hijack())

@override
dangerous routine FrozenController.destroy()
  if me.strong_count() == 0
    me.hijack().invalidate()
    return
  return
